# Bright.Experience — Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in your values

# ── Required ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Supabase — Project connection
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key-here

# Site URL — canonical public URL used for email links, Supabase auth
# redirects (password reset, magic links), and Open Graph metadata.
# Set to your Vercel/production domain in production.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Supabase service-role key — required for cron jobs, invites, and any
# query that needs to bypass RLS. Treat it like a database password:
# never expose to the browser, never commit.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key-here

# ── Required in production ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# checkRequiredEnv() in src/lib/env.ts throws at boot when any of these is
# missing and NODE_ENV=production, so a misconfigured deploy fails loudly
# instead of half-working. Development only warns. The full list:
#
#   CRON_SECRET                — see Cron below; without it every cron 401s
#   RESEND_API_KEY, FROM_EMAIL — see Resend below; without them no email sends
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CALCOM_LINK    — see Cal.com below
#   BRIGHTBLUE_WEBHOOK_SECRET  — see Bright.Blue Cloud below
#   CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET      — see Cal.com below

# ── Recommended ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Resend — Transactional email for notifications, digests, and invites.
# When unset, emails are logged to the console instead.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Sign up at https://resend.com and verify a sending domain
#   2. Create an API key and paste it below
#   3. (Optional) Override the from/team addresses
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxx
# FROM_EMAIL=noreply@brightblue.co.uk
# STUDIO_TEAM_EMAIL=studio@brightblue.co.uk
# SALES_TEAM_EMAIL=sales@brightblue.co.uk

# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Mock mode — when "true", use the in-memory mock Supabase dataset
# (src/lib/supabase/mock/) instead of a live project. Ideal for UI demos
# and offline work. Leave unset / "false" for real auth, RLS, and storage.
#
# Do NOT set this here. It lives in `.env.development`, which Next.js loads only
# under `next dev`, and a production build ignores the variable outright: mock
# mode accepts any password and bypasses RLS, so honouring it in production
# would mean no authentication at all. See src/lib/supabase/mock/flag.ts.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MOCK_MODE=true
#
# Escape hatch for the E2E harness only, which builds in production mode with no
# Postgres to point at. Setting this on a real deployment disables auth.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ALLOW_INSECURE_MOCK_AUTH=1

# Test-mode endpoints (/api/test/*) — programmatic persona sign-in for
# Playwright. Personas map onto the users created by supabase/seed-users.ts, so
# the shared demo password applies unless TEST_USER_PASSWORD overrides it.
#
# A production build routes /api/test/* to a 404 regardless of TEST_MODE. The
# E2E harness builds in production mode, so it sets ALLOW_TEST_AUTH_ROUTES too.
# Never set that second variable on a real deployment: together they let anyone
# POST their way into a session.
# TEST_MODE=1
# TEST_USER_PASSWORD=demo-password-123
# ALLOW_TEST_AUTH_ROUTES=1

# Booking auto-provision — when "true", an accepted proposal or a book-now
# submission immediately spins up a real account, event workspace and customer
# invite. Leave UNSET for demos/sandboxes so funnel walkthroughs land as
# in-flight quotes an internal user converts deliberately (no throwaway events,
# no real invite emails). Set to "true" only in production.
# BOOKING_AUTO_PROVISION=true

# Public API + outbound webhooks — the /admin/api surface that mints `bb_…`
# keys and webhook subscriptions. OFF by default, because nothing authenticates
# with a key yet and nothing delivers to a subscription URL: issuing either
# would hand a partner a credential for an API that never answers. The page
# 404s, the nav item is hidden, and the mint actions refuse while it is unset.
# Revoking an existing key always works. Turn this on once the API and the
# dispatcher exist. (Inbound Cal.com / Bright.Blue webhooks are unaffected.)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PUBLIC_API_ENABLED=1

# Proxy hops in front of the app, for rate-limit IP resolution. Default 0 uses
# the right-most `X-Forwarded-For` entry — the address our own edge observed —
# so a caller who prepends a fake address gets the same bucket every time.
# Raise this by one for each additional trusted proxy you put in front; setting
# it too high starts trusting caller-supplied values again. Platform headers
# (x-vercel-forwarded-for, cf-connecting-ip) win outright and ignore this.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# Cron — shared secret protecting /api/cron/* routes. Vercel Cron sends
# an `Authorization: Bearer ${CRON_SECRET}` header on every invocation when
# this is set; any request without a matching bearer gets a 401. (The old
# `x-vercel-cron` header bypass was removed — it was spoofable.)
# Generate a long random string and add it to your Vercel project settings.
# CRON_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-long-random-string

# Bright.Blue Cloud — live telemetry & webhooks (degrades gracefully)
# Outbound API: pulls live snapshots and post-show reports from Cloud
# BRIGHTBLUE_API_URL=https://cloud.bright.blue/api/v1
# BRIGHTBLUE_API_KEY=your-api-key-here
# Inbound webhooks: Cloud pushes telemetry, leads, heartbeats, and reports
# BRIGHTBLUE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=shared-hmac-secret-here

# Sentry — error observability. When the DSN is unset, capture calls are
# no-ops. The runtime SDK (client/server/edge) reads NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://xxxxx@oXXXX.ingest.sentry.io/XXXX
#
# Build-time source-map upload (next.config.ts -> withSentryConfig) needs the
# org/project slugs plus an auth token. Without them the build still succeeds
# but stack traces stay minified. Set these in CI / Vercel project settings.
# SENTRY_ORG=your-sentry-org-slug
# SENTRY_PROJECT=your-sentry-project-slug
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=sntrys_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# File scanning — malware screen for uploaded creative assets. When unset,
# uploads are accepted without scanning (local/dev/pre-handoff). Point this at
# a ClamAV REST shim or cloud AV endpoint that accepts raw bytes and returns
# JSON { clean: boolean, signature?: string }.
# FILE_SCAN_URL=https://scanner.internal/scan
# FILE_SCAN_TOKEN=optional-bearer-token

# Cal.com — walkthrough call scheduling (degrades gracefully).
# When NEXT_PUBLIC_CALCOM_LINK is set, customer surfaces render the inline
# Cal.com booker (each AE connects their own Google Calendar inside Cal.com,
# so availability + booked calls sync natively). When unset, the built-in
# preset slot picker is used instead — ideal for demos.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Create a 15-minute event type at https://cal.com (or your self-hosted
#      instance) and paste its path below, e.g. "brightblue/15min"
#   2. In Cal.com → Settings → Developer → Webhooks, add
#      https://<your-domain>/api/webhooks/calcom with triggers
#      BOOKING_CREATED, BOOKING_RESCHEDULED, BOOKING_CANCELLED, MEETING_ENDED,
#      and set a secret. Paste the same secret below.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CALCOM_LINK=brightblue/15min
# CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=shared-hmac-secret-here

# Pipedrive — CRM write-back integration (no-op if unset).
# The token also lives in pipedrive_config.api_token once
# /admin/integrations/pipedrive is set up; this env override exists
# for local development.
# PIPEDRIVE_API_TOKEN=
# PIPEDRIVE_BASE_URL=https://api.pipedrive.com
# Web app base URL for the "Open in Pipedrive" deal chip.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PIPEDRIVE_WEB_BASE_URL=https://app.pipedrive.com
